<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Indigenous Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning About Our Diverse Cultures</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate both fiction and non-fiction stories. Interactive: Conduct interviews on personal interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a story about an Indigenous festival in Panama's comarcas, including sensory details (e.g., "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we saw woven chaquiras, heard traditional flute music, and watched the dancers perform their ancestral steps." "During the Guna Yala celebration, we admired the vibrant molas with intricate patterns and listened to chants while the community shared stories of their ancestors." "At the Emberá cultural festival, we saw beautifully painted jaguá designs on the dancers' skin, heard the rhythmic beat of the drums, and tasted freshly cooked potatoes."). Can conduct a brief interview about a peer's cultural background (e.g., "What traditions does your family celebrate?"). Can describe the history of an indigenous group in class discussions (e.g., "I'm interested in learning about the Guna people's history").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various vivid images of different Indigenous festivals in Panama (e.g., Ngäbe-Buglé, Guna Yala, Emberá). Students work in small groups and describe what they see, hear, and imagine. Teacher prompts: "Look at these images of traditional festivals. What <b>colors</b> do you see in the clothing or <b>art</b>? What kind of music do you think they play? What do you notice about their unique <b>patterns</b> or <b>customs</b>?" Students share their observations orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of a Guna Mola. "Look at this mola. It's very <b>vibrant</b> and has intricate <b>patterns</b>. The Guna people have a rich <b>heritage</b> of making these. They <b>preserve</b> their **traditions** through this beautiful **art**." The teacher models the pronunciation, specifically focusing on the stress in "<b>vibrant</b>" and "<b>patterns</b>," and explains the meaning of "<b>heritage</b>" and "<b>preserve</b>" in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are molas a modern or an <b>ancestral</b> art form?" (Expected answer: An ancestral art form).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'preserve' mean to keep something safe, or to throw it away?" (Expected answer: To keep something safe).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Teacher presents a short narrative excerpt (audio or read aloud by teacher) describing a visit to an Indigenous festival in Panama's comarcas, incorporating sensory details, historical facts (e.g., "Gunas migrated in the 16th century"), and showcasing adjectives (e.g., "colorful traditional clothing"). Also, a sample interview dialogue about a peer's cultural background.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Speaking: Expressing opinions, giving information, and engaging in simple conversations about cultural heritage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening to the narrative, in pairs, students discuss: "Based on the story, what did the narrator see, hear, and taste at the festival? What <b>traditions</b> did they mention?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the interview dialogue, students discuss: "What questions did the interviewer ask about the person's <b>cultural background</b>? What did the person share about their <b>family roots</b>?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using Present Simple and Past Simple for describing traditions and historical facts, adjectives for detailed descriptions (e.g., "colorful traditional clothing," "vibrant molas"), and Present Perfect for ongoing preservation efforts (e.g., "They have preserved traditions."). Key vocabulary: <b>Indigenous, cultures, traditions, vibrant, patterns, preserve, heritage, ancestry, community, cultural background, family roots</b>. Pronunciation focus on stress in "Indigenous," "ancestry," "migration," "identity," "vibrant," and "handmade."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion - Oral):</b> The teacher provides incomplete sentences related to Indigenous cultures on the board or screen. Students work in pairs to orally complete them using target vocabulary and appropriate grammar.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Guna people create ______ (vibrant/traditional/preserved) molas with intricate ______ (patterns/customs/history)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Last year, I learned that the Emberá people ______ (migrated/celebrated/protected) their ______ (land/language/identity) for centuries."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Indigenous ______ (communities/heritage/diversity) in Panama ______ (have preserved/are preserving/preserve) their unique ______ (heritage/migration/knowledge) for generations." (Teacher reminds students to stress "preserved" and "heritage" correctly).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Guided Q&A):</b> The teacher leads a Q&A session. Students ask and answer questions in pairs, using provided prompts and incorporating adjectives and specific tenses.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "Ngäbe-Buglé / chaquiras / colorful" -> Student A: "What are Ngäbe-Buglé chaquiras like?" Student B: "They are very <b>colorful</b> and often <b>handmade</b>." (Teacher models stress on "colorful," "handmade").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "Guna Yala / history / protect land" -> Student A: "How have the Guna people <b>protected</b> their <b>land</b>?" Student B: "They <b>have protected</b> their <b>land</b> through their <b>ancestral</b> laws." (Focus on present perfect and adjective).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "Emberá / jaguá designs / vibrant" -> Student A: "Can you describe Emberá jaguá designs?" Student B: "They are <b>vibrant</b> and tell stories of their <b>ancestry</b> and **identity**."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Sentence Expansion):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A says a simple sentence about an Indigenous culture (e.g., "The Guna celebrate"). Student B expands it using an adjective and a past/present perfect tense verb, incorporating details and pragmatic phrases. (e.g., "Yes, they <b>celebrate vibrant ceremonies</b> and <b>have preserved</b> their <b>customs</b> for a long time. The Guna people's <b>customs</b> are unique and valuable to our nation.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Cultural Heritage Interview and Story Sharing."</b> Students work in pairs to perform a two-part speaking task focusing on their learning outcome.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Interview:</b> Student A interviews Student B about their family's <b>cultural background</b> or <b>traditions</b> (real or imagined). Student A must use at least three questions incorporating target vocabulary. Suggested questions: "What <b>traditions</b> does your family <b>celebrate</b>?" "Do you have any special <b>customs</b> or <b>family roots</b>?" "How have your family's <b>traditions</b> been <b>preserved</b> over time?" Student B answers, incorporating vocabulary (<b>traditions, customs, family roots, cultural background, preserve</b>), grammar (Present Simple, Past Simple, Present Perfect), and adjectives. They might also use pragmatic phrases like "Groups like the Emberá have lived here for centuries and maintain traditional ways."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Story Narration:</b> After the interview, Student B narrates a short (3-4 sentences) story about an <b>Indigenous</b> festival in Panama's comarcas, either real or fictional. The story must include specific sensory details as per the learning outcome (e.g., "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we saw woven <b>chaquiras</b>, heard <b>traditional</b> flute music, and watched the dancers perform their <b>ancestral</b> steps." "During the Guna Yala celebration, we admired the <b>vibrant</b> <b>molas</b> with intricate <b>patterns</b> and listened to chants while the <b>community</b> shared stories of their <b>ancestors</b>."). They should also attempt to use pragmatic phrases like "The Guna people's <b>customs</b> are unique and valuable to our nation."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students then swap roles. The focus is on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, ensuring the use of target vocabulary, grammar, and appropriate pronunciation (stress on "Indigenous," "ancestry," "identity," "vibrant").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Cultural Heritage Interview and Story Sharing" activity, focusing on fluency and use of target language. Additionally, students use a simple peer assessment rubric to evaluate their partner's use of vocabulary and clarity during the task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their interview and story narration for the teacher or a small peer group, demonstrating their ability to narrate with sensory details and conduct a brief interview.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of multisyllabic words like "<b>Indigenous</b>," "<b>ancestry</b>," "<b>identity</b>," "<b>vibrant</b>").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and varied use of target vocabulary (e.g., "<b>heritage</b>," "<b>preserve</b>," "<b>vibrant</b>," "<b>patterns</b>," "<b>customs</b>," "<b>cultural background</b>").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Present Simple and Past Simple for descriptions and narration, and Present Perfect for ongoing actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of specific sensory details in story narration.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in expressing ideas and answering questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active listening during the interview portion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to use pragmatic expressions (e.g., "The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new vocabulary did you use today to describe <b>Indigenous cultures</b>?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one thing you learned about your partner's <b>cultural background</b> or an <b>Indigenous</b> festival?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How confident do I feel narrating a story about an <b>Indigenous</b> festival in English? What parts were easy, and what was challenging (e.g., using sensory details, remembering specific grammar)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did I conduct an interview and ask questions about <b>cultural backgrounds</b>? What could I improve for next time to make my questions or answers clearer?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today, students actively engaged in both interviews and short narrations, successfully using the target vocabulary and grammar. The focus on sensory details helped them narrate more vividly. Some students still need more practice with irregular past verbs and consistent use of the present perfect. The discussions about "How have communities in Panama worked to <b>preserve Indigenous cultures</b>?" were effective in integrating sociolinguistic competence. For the next lesson, I will scaffold sentence construction by providing sentence starters and focusing on descriptive adjectives to help them transfer their spoken narratives into written form. I will also ensure they can clearly describe <b>patterns</b> and <b>art</b> in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, we will focus on <b>Writing Composition</b>. Students will use the vocabulary, grammar, and descriptive language practiced today to write a paragraph about an <b>Indigenous</b> festival or a significant cultural aspect, incorporating the sensory details and historical facts we discussed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>